# tools/dead-conditionals-ledger -- what tools/dead-conditionals is allowed to
# find. Read that script's header first; it says what each row means and what
# makes one stale.
#
# TAB-SEPARATED. Two kinds of row:
#
#   allow	NAME	why this identifier is legitimately conditional here
#   pending	FILE	NAME	who removes it, and when
#
# BOTH ARE CHECKED FOR STALENESS, because an allowlist nobody re-reads is the
# same trap as no allowlist at all:
#
#   * an `allow` name that every shim project's command line defines is stale
#     -- the guard is live and must not be waived;
#   * an `allow` name no shim file conditions on any more is stale;
#   * a `pending` site that has been cleaned up is stale.
#
# So the pending list can only shrink. A file ported in Phase 7 carrying a dead
# mod guard has no row here and fails naming the file and the define, which is
# what this whole mechanism is for.
#
#=============================================================================
# ALLOW -- legitimately conditional in THIS build.
#=============================================================================
#
# PORTABILITY, NOT MOD GUARDS, and the distinction is the whole point of having
# an allowlist. WIN32 is dead here only in the sense that this build targets
# linux64; the surrounding SDK still carries Windows, and deleting the guard
# would discard that portability in silence. A mod guard like TF_CLIENT_DLL is
# dead in a different way -- this is not that game and never will be -- and is
# NOT allowed here, which is what dead_conditionals_test.cpp pins.
allow	WIN32	portability: a Windows configuration of this same tree defines it; this build is linux64 only. Removing the guard would discard Windows support the surrounding SDK still carries.
allow	_WIN32	portability: the compiler's own Windows spelling of the same thing, on the same reasoning as WIN32.
#
# SIDE MARKERS. A file in sourcesharp/game/shared is compiled into BOTH the
# client and the server, and CLIENT_DLL / GAME_DLL is how it tells which one it
# is in. Each is on exactly one of the two command lines, so from the other
# side's compile it looks undefined -- correctly, and by construction.
allow	CLIENT_DLL	side marker: on the client's command line and not the server's. A shared file conditioning on it is correct by construction.
allow	GAME_DLL	side marker: on the server's command line and not the client's, the mirror of CLIENT_DLL.
allow	NDEBUG	configuration marker: the release preset defines it (SS_DEFS_release, cmake/SourceSharp.cmake) and debug does not, so a scan of the debug build sees it as never defined. convar_port.cpp keeps stock tier1's `#ifndef NDEBUG` for development-only convars, which differs by configuration on purpose.
#
# OTHER CONFIGURATIONS OF THIS SAME TREE. These are set by a build that really
# exists and is really run -- just not the shipping one -- so the conditional
# has a live arm somewhere and is not dead code.
# SS_CBASEENTITY_ALIAS HAD A ROW HERE AND THE FLIP MADE IT STALE. Its reason was
# "another configuration of this tree: the armed alias survey defines it" -- true
# while the alias was a survey. THE FLIP ARMED IT IN BOTH server_csharp.vpc AND
# client_csharp.vpc, so it is on every shim project's command line and there is
# no other configuration to waive for. tools/dead-conditionals said so itself:
# "STALE allow row: no shim file conditions on SS_CBASEENTITY_ALIAS any more --
# delete the row". A waiver is not a fact; removing it makes the check stricter,
# which is the direction this file's own header says the list may move.
allow	SS_COVERAGE	another configuration of this tree: the coverage build defines it.
# PHASE 6B, FOR A RULING: the two link-mode rows below were waived as "other
# VPC configurations" of this tree. VPC is retired, and neither VPC's own graph
# (main at 8e35279) nor the CMake build defines _STATIC_LINKED or _SHARED_LIB on
# any target, so no configuration that is built here gives these guards a live
# arm. They stay waived, unchanged, until someone rules whether the sites go.
allow	_STATIC_LINKED	link mode: set by the static-link VPC configurations in this tree, not by the shared-object one this builds.
allow	_SHARED_LIB	link mode: the mirror of _STATIC_LINKED, set by other VPC configurations here.
allow	SS_NO_CRT	another configuration of this tree: ss_sdk_target's NO_RUNTIME sets it on MSVC only (cmake/SourceSharp.cmake), for a library linked with no CRT, so a scan of the Linux build sees it as never defined. fwd_physics.cpp defines the one CRT handler the forwarder libraries' vtables name (_purecall) under it; a test compiling the same source into an executable with a CRT leaves it to the CRT.
allow	SS_FWD_SERVER_BUILD	side marker: on libss_fwd_server's command line and not libss_fwd_client's (src/fwd/native/CMakeLists.txt). fwd_physics.cpp is a source of both libraries, so the scan of the client's compile sees the server's marker as never defined, the way a shared file sees GAME_DLL.
allow	SS_FWD_CLIENT_BUILD	side marker: the mirror of SS_FWD_SERVER_BUILD, on libss_fwd_client's command line and not the server library's.
#
#=============================================================================
# PENDING -- dead here, already present when this check landed, with an owner.
#
# None of these is lane 5b-split's file. The lane brief is explicit that a lane
# touches only what its step owns, so each is written down rather than edited:
# the row makes the site visible, stops the count growing, and fails the moment
# somebody cleans it up and forgets to delete the row.
#=============================================================================
pending	sourcesharp/common/steamid.cpp	HL1	relocated stock, verbatim (Steam's own steamclientpublic.h lineage). Owned by whoever next touches the relocation.
pending	sourcesharp/common/steamid.cpp	STEAM	relocated stock, verbatim. Same owner as HL1 above.
pending	sourcesharp/public/dt_send.cpp	SUPPORTS_INT64	relocated stock, verbatim. Owned by the datatable work, not by a client-shell lane.
pending	sourcesharp/game/server/shell_engineboundary.cpp	TF_DLL	server shim, and a mod guard: this is not Team Fortress. Owned by the server side.
